<h1>China Doesn't Build Businesses. Sales Do.</h1>

<p><strong>27 June 2026</strong></p>

<hr>

<p>

When I was in China, most people thought my focus was factories,
products, exhibitions, or supplier meetings.

<br><br>

But the truth is different.

<br><br>

60% of my focus was on selling.

<br><br>

While walking through markets in Shenzhen, sitting in factories in Dongguan,
or traveling across China, my mind was still in India.

<br><br>

Calling customers.<br>
Replying to inquiries.<br>
Negotiating deals.<br>
Following up on payments.<br>
Closing orders.

<br><br>

Because factories don't pay you.<br>
Products don't pay you.<br>
Supplier visits don't pay you.<br>
Customers pay you.

<br><br>

Many entrepreneurs spend months searching for the perfect product,
perfect supplier, perfect packaging, and perfect logo.

<br><br>

But they forget the most important thing.

<br><br>

Someone has to buy it.

<br><br>

If sales stop, everything stops.

<br><br>

No matter where you are in the world,
your business grows when you stay close to customers.

<br><br>

Factories create products.

<br><br>

Sales create businesses.

</p>

